now uh  moving on to the u.n as we reported just  a few moments ago uh the u.n general  assembly voted on that resolution  demanding that russia withdraw its  troops from ukraine and as you heard 141  voted in favor five against russia and  belarus uh among those five ahead of  that vote russia faced urgent calls to  end its assault on ukraine but  moscow still denies that it's targeting  civilians  they have come to the ukrainian soil  not only to kill some of us not only to  shift our course and priorities  they have come to deprive ukraine of the  very right to exist  they have come to resolve the ukrainian  issue  as their propagandists claimed  just stop and think  whether these wars remind you of  something  today we call on russia  to stop its unprovoked  unjustified  unconscionable war  we call on russia  to respect the sovereignty  and territorial integrity  of ukraine  we call on another neighbor of ukraine  belarus who you've just heard from  to stop supporting  the war and stop allowing its territory  to be used to facilitate this aggression  we are not carrying out strikes on  civilian facilities or civilians  don't believe in the large number of  fake news being spread around the  internet on this  peaceful scenarios of ukraine could  happen  if the radicals are concerned about  preserving civilian lives in their  country  rather than hiding behind them there is  no reference to this call on the  resolution for us to vote on  russia's advance on the capital kiev  appears to have stalled for the moment  that's according to u.s officials it's  believed that 80 of russia's combat  power that was assembled along the  border is now in the country president  putin mobilized 190 000 troops according  to estimates ukraine has 209 000 active  duty soldiers and last week president  zielinski activated thousands more  reservists to support them joining us  now from washington dc uh lauren  esperanza who's director of the  transatlantic defense and security  program at the center for european  policy analysis good to have you with us  uh lauren do you agree with that  assessment that russia's advance on the  capital has stalled or is are we are we  seeing as a change in  tactics by russian military going on  here right now  well thanks for having me so i think we  saw  originally a russian military that was  not well organized uh that did not have  sufficient logistics and support they  they suffered some early failures  running out of fuel and food and we're  still seeing some of some of those  failures on the ground and so i think  that is why we're hearing these reports  of maybe that convoy being stalled and  those those failures are real but what  we're starting to see is russia also  shifting its military tactics because of  those failures and it's resorting to  more of its traditional way of war which  basically involves applying massive  firepower to major civilian population  centers and we are seeing uh the use of  artillery and rockets and missiles uh on  on major cities like kharkiv and and we  saw the tv uh tower being struck down in  kiev yesterday  and i think you know despite the the  russian claims that they are not  striking civilian targets i mean we have  evidence in reporting that directly  contradicts that i mean we have seen  everything from a university to a  maternity clinic to our apartment  buildings being bombed and the reality  is that this this conflict is this war  is getting bloodier and brutal uh  more brutal by the second and and i  think unfortunately that's a sign of  what's more to come and this is really  turning into a humanitarian disaster so  what is russia's end game though what is  it they're trying to do militarily here  sure so a lot of what we're seeing is  that they're trying to advance towards  kiev i think this is a big center of the  of the war that they are trying to  surround and encircle the city  and and and siege the city to to topple  the government and hopefully  they 